#: ../../using/backup.rst:2 70978cc1dda9490f9a3317e0eed7f0cb
msgid "Writing a backup to a tape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:4 87f47bf0614c43069874e0dcac1b164f
msgid ""
"The console gives you a library; what writes to it is ``tar``, Bacula, "
"NetBackup, or whatever you already use. This is the smallest complete "
"example: put a cartridge in a drive, write to it, read it back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:8 60de3be33c0647ad9fa3d6339aab884a
msgid ""
"Everything below was run against library 30, an STK L80 with four T10000B"
" drives."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:16 5a7cd82e6da54c548448645ab66a84ec
msgid "1. Find the drive's device"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:18 a02b919760b24abc81848c969c90eae1
msgid ""
"Backup software writes to a device node, not to a library. Ask which node"
" a drive is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:34 e31f38ca74064b909663eb617b5f085f
msgid ""
"``/dev/nst5`` is the *non-rewinding* node, which is the one to use: "
"closing ``/dev/st5`` rewinds the tape, so a second ``tar`` would write "
"over the first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:37 8419c66292bb43978fb9b77ba232349c
msgid "``mhvtl scsi map`` lists every library and drive with its nodes at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:40 5ff72076fca84954a974c3648e50b283
msgid "2. Put a cartridge in the drive"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:54 6f2396721d144ea483527dd326477bc9
msgid ""
"The ``0`` is the robot's drive number, counting from zero - not the drive"
" id. ``mhvtl status library 30`` shows both."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:57 a6e2f22ad5a34fb299d45ffdb11c31aa
msgid "In the console this is **The robot → Mount a tape** on the library page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:60 5b9af361f0b241c4a5c7c1837f1a201c
msgid "3. Write"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:67 83ab878b3bca4c828030ba1610c63f06
msgid ""
"``-b 128`` writes 64 KB records, which suits tape far better than tar's "
"default of 10 KB. The leading-slash warning is tar being careful, not a "
"problem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:71 2529b60500ed4a4cbccabfc97db473c6
msgid "While it runs, the drive can be watched - see :doc:`watching`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:82 906938502e224cc9a7aee91a12ab54f1
msgid "4. Read it back"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:94 5906560f0fc046c1853b54667d1d7ce1
msgid ""
"A restore is ``tar -xf`` with the same block size. Rewind first: a tape "
"is a position as much as a medium, and ``tar`` reads from wherever the "
"head is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:98 806178593a8943149dc5ab1e2d0064d9
msgid "5. Put the cartridge away"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:107 d4c9fa545cc044b0a8bc2776dd44985b
msgid "Several backups on one tape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:109 5582904798e44ce09e381e971fda26f3
msgid ""
"Each ``tar`` writes a file mark and stops. Writing again from where the "
"last one ended puts a second archive on the same cartridge - which is "
"what the non-rewinding node is for:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:118 967c9c68331341c7b373a6b595d90559
msgid "To read the second, skip the first:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:127 7c28d2f7d97b487a93f5be478edc7dfc
msgid "Why the tape says it used no megabytes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:129 451a8889e18445a6aa0da49d579349da
msgid ""
"The bash documentation is about half a megabyte, and ``USED MB`` counts "
"whole megabytes, so a small backup shows as ``0``. The tape's files are "
"really there:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../using/backup.rst:140 70e6c1a4494a4926bfc195c516dca815
msgid ""
"MHVTL compresses what it writes - this backup compressed 2.56 to 1 - so a"
" cartridge holds more than its size in data, and ``used`` is the "
"compressed figure, because that is what occupies the disk."
msgstr ""

